# pihole-peek — category rules for the html report.
#
# The script knows nothing about categories: everything the report shows comes
# from this file. Edit it, and the next report follows.
#
#   <category> <regex>            classify a domain; the FIRST match wins, so
#                                 the order of the lines is the specification
#   @color <category> <light> [dark]
#                                 the dot beside the name; without it the dot
#                                 stays neutral and the written name carries
#                                 the meaning on its own
#   @about <category> <text>      the sentence shown in the legend
#
# The regex is case insensitive and it is tested against the whole domain.
# A line whose first non-blank character is '#' is a comment. Anywhere else a
# '#' belongs to the value, so a regex or a colour can hold one.
#
# To add rules of your own, do NOT edit this file — a later `git pull` would
# fight you. Write them in "categories.local" next to it: that file is ignored
# by git and its rules are tried BEFORE these, so they always win.
#
# Colours: only four categories are coloured, and tracking and telemetry share
# one hue because they mean the same thing — data about the device leaving the
# network. Three hues is what clears the colour-blindness and contrast checks
# on both the light and the dark surface; a fourth would not. Every other
# category keeps a neutral dot, which scales to any number of categories.

# ---- colours -------------------------------------------------------------
@color ads           #eb6834 #d95926
@color acr           #1baf7a #199e70
@color tracking      #2a78d6 #3987e5
@color telemetry     #2a78d6 #3987e5

# ---- what each category means -------------------------------------------
@about ads           ad exchanges and ad SDKs
@about acr           automatic content recognition: what is on the screen
@about tracking      third-party analytics, attribution and monitoring
@about telemetry     logs, metrics, beacons and crash reports of the device itself
@about connectivity  captive portal and "am I online" checks
@about pki           certificate validation: OCSP, CRL, timestamping
@about ntp           clock synchronisation
@about dns           public resolvers, DNS over HTTPS and DNS services
@about update        firmware, packages and mirrors
@about smarthome     connected devices and home automation
@about gaming        game platforms and stores
@about social        social networks and messaging
@about ai            assistants and language models
@about shopping      shops and marketplaces
@about streaming     the media services themselves
@about vendor        services of the device maker
@about cdn           content delivery networks
@about cloud         generic cloud, hosting and platform APIs
@about other         no rule matched this domain

